= Consistency — Simple =

A logical system is '''consistent''' when its rules do not let it prove a contradiction in the relevant sense.

In ordinary classical logic, a system is inconsistent if it can prove both a statement and its opposite.

Consistency does not mean every statement in the system is true.

A theory can be internally consistent and still describe the real world badly.

Consistency is also different from completeness. A consistent theory may still leave some questions undecided.

